/*
RMSTEST_30 - does grouped_by_team space TEAMS evenly, or space PLAYERS evenly
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 15

QUESTION
guide:356 pins the intra-team spacing exactly, at double the base_size, and says
nothing at all about what happens to the ring itself. Two readings survive:

  A  Teams are spaced evenly around the ring, and each team's members are
     packed together at their team's slot. Sec.6.1 models this one.
  B  Players are spaced evenly around the ring and merely REORDERED so that
     teammates end up adjacent.

At 4v4 the two are nearly indistinguishable, which is exactly why this has gone
unresolved - 4v4 is the configuration anyone would test by hand. They diverge
sharply at UNEVEN team sizes, and that is what this run is for.

METHOD
create_player_lands with grouped_by_team and a base_size large enough that the
intra-team spacing is unambiguous. Each player land is one 4-connected GRASS
patch, so the eight centroids are the eight origins, and the matrix of angular
gaps between consecutive origins answers the question directly. This is the same
instrument and the same shape of question as RMSTEST_18's spacing matrix, which
is why it is cheap.

THREE CONFIGURATIONS, and the third is the one that decides it:
  30-A  4v4
  30-B  2v2v2v2
  30-C  5v1v1v1   <- the discriminator

TEAMS ARE SET OUTSIDE THE SCRIPT, in the lobby or the editor's player settings.
Read RMSTEST_29's note on this before starting; if the editor cannot set teams
for a random map, both 29 and 30 need a real lobby.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH 8 PLAYERS, all three configurations.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. 30-C under reading A: one dense arc of five origins with tight, equal gaps,
   then three lone origins spread across the rest of the circle. Gap
   distribution is strongly BIMODAL - a few small intra-team gaps and a few
   large inter-team ones.
2. 30-C under reading B: eight origins at near-uniform 45 degree gaps, with the
   five teammates simply happening to be consecutive. Gap distribution is
   UNIMODAL and flat.
   These two predictions do not overlap, so one run at 5v1v1v1 settles it.
3. 30-A should look almost identical under both readings. It is included as the
   sanity case, NOT as evidence - if 30-A and 30-C disagree about which reading
   holds, trust 30-C.
4. 30-B distinguishes a subtler thing: under A, four evenly spaced pairs; under
   B, eight evenly spaced singles. The gap sequence should alternate
   small-large-small-large under A and be constant under B.

ALSO UNANSWERED, and this run may show it for free
What happens when the packed groups do not fit the circumference. It is
reachable on a small map with a large base_size, and 30-C on Normal with
base_size 8 puts five lands into one arc. If the five-team arc wraps into its
neighbours, or if origins start landing on top of each other, that is the
overflow behaviour showing itself and it is worth recording even though nothing
currently models it.

WATCH FOR
Patch count must be 8. Packed teammates are the most likely lands in this batch
to merge into a single patch, and a merged patch reports one centroid sitting
between two real origins - see RMSTEST_25. If teams merge at base_size 8, drop
number_of_tiles rather than base_size, since base_size is what sets the spacing
being measured.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  Convert each centroid to an angle about the map center (99.5, 99.5 on a 200
  map), sort, and take consecutive differences. That gap sequence is the answer.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  grouped_by_team

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    number_of_tiles 200
    base_size 8
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
